        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Base Envelope Creation \u0026 Validation Flow\n├── envelope.OK() constructor \u003c-- 1a\n│   ├── Build Envelope struct \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:145\n│   │   ├── Version = 1 \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:146\n│   │   ├── Status = \"ok\" \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:147\n│   │   ├── Command = command param \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:148\n│   │   ├── Data = data param \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:149\n│   │   └── Meta initialization \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:150\n│   │       └── TS = RFC3339 UTC \u003c-- 1b\n│   ├── Apply options (opts...) \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:154\n│   └── applyMetaDefaults() \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:157\n│\n└── envelope.Validate() \u003c-- 1c\n    ├── Check version == 1 \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:191\n    ├── Check command non-empty \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:194\n    ├── Status-based validation \u003c-- 1d\n    │   ├── if status == \"ok\" \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:198\n    │   │   └── Error fields must be empty \u003c-- 1e\n    │   └── if status == \"error\" \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:202\n    │       ├── Error.Code required \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:203\n    │       └── Error.Message required \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:206\n    └── Timestamp validation \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:212\n        ├── Parse RFC3339 format \u003c-- 1f\n        └── Verify UTC timezone \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:220",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nEvery command in foxctl returns a **JSON envelope** - a standardized wrapper that ensures consistent, machine-parseable output across all operations. Without this structure, LLMs and automation tools would struggle to reliably parse command results, distinguish success from failure, or extract metadata like timestamps and execution sources. The envelope system solves this by enforcing **Core Profile v1** invariants: every response must have a version, status, command name, timestamp, and proper error fields when things fail [1c].\n\n## Details\n\n### Envelope Construction\n\nCreating a success envelope starts with `envelope.OK()` [1a], which builds an `Envelope` struct with:\n- **Version 1** (the only supported version)\n- **Status \"ok\"** to indicate success\n- The **command name** (e.g., \"foxctl.run\")\n- A **data payload** (arbitrary JSON-compatible data)\n- **Meta block** with an RFC3339 UTC timestamp [1b]\n\nThe constructor applies any provided options and ensures metadata defaults are set before returning the envelope.\n\n### Validation Rules\n\nThe `envelope.Validate()` function [1c] enforces Core Profile v1 rules:\n\n1. **Version must be 1** - rejects any other version number\n2. **Command must be non-empty** - every envelope needs a command identifier\n3. **Status-specific rules** [1d]:\n   - **OK envelopes** must have empty error fields [1e] - you can't have both success and error information\n   - **Error envelopes** must have both `error.code` and `error.message` populated\n4. **Timestamp requirements**:\n   - Must be valid RFC3339 format [1f]\n   - Must be in UTC timezone (not local time)\n\nThese validation rules ensure that **every envelope is unambiguous**: you can always tell if an operation succeeded or failed, when it happened, and what went wrong if it failed. This deterministic structure is what makes foxctl suitable as a \"bash for LLMs.\""
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "title": "Protocol-Level Validation Extensions",
      "description": "Protocol package - extends base validation with CAS digest, cache metadata, memory metadata, and error status code checks",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "2a",
          "path": "internal/protocol/protocol.go",
          "lineNumber": 135,
          "lineContent": "func Validate(env envelope.Envelope) error {",
          "title": "Protocol Validation Entry",
          "description": "Top-level validation that wraps base validation and adds protocol-level checks"
        },
        {
          "id": "2b",
          "path": "internal/protocol/protocol.go",
          "lineNumber": 137,
          "lineContent": "if err := envelope.Validate(env); err != nil {",
          "title": "Base Validation Invocation",
          "description": "Calls core envelope validation first before protocol extensions"
        },
        {
          "id": "2c",
          "path": "internal/protocol/protocol.go",
          "lineNumber": 142,
          "lineContent": "if err := validateCASDigest(env); err != nil {",
          "title": "CAS Digest Validation",
          "description": "Ensures meta.cas_digest matches data.artifact field when present"
        },
        {
          "id": "2d",
          "path": "internal/protocol/protocol.go",
          "lineNumber": 146,
          "lineContent": "if err := validateCacheMetadata(env); err != nil {",
          "title": "Cache Metadata Validation",
          "description": "Validates cache source requires cache_key in meta"
        },
        {
          "id": "2e",
          "path": "internal/protocol/protocol.go",
          "lineNumber": 150,
          "lineContent": "if err := validateMemoryMetadata(env); err != nil {",
          "title": "Memory Metadata Validation",
          "description": "Validates memory source requires memory reference with name and type"
        },
        {
          "id": "2f",
          "path": "internal/protocol/protocol.go",
          "lineNumber": 154,
          "lineContent": "if err := validateErrorStatusCode(env); err != nil {",
          "title": "Error Status Code Validation",
          "description": "Ensures error envelopes with status_code have values in 400-599 range"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Protocol Validation System (Trace 2)\n└── protocol.Validate() entry \u003c-- 2a\n    ├── envelope.Validate() base checks \u003c-- 2b\n    │   ├── Version check (must be 1) \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:191\n    │   ├── Status check (ok|error) \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:197\n    │   ├── Command non-empty check \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:194\n    │   ├── Timestamp RFC3339 UTC check \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:216\n    │   └── Error fields consistency check \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:198\n    └── Protocol-level extensions\n        ├── validateCASDigest() \u003c-- 2c\n        │   ├── extractArtifactValue() \u003c-- internal/protocol/protocol.go:164\n        │   └── Compare meta.cas_digest to data.artifact \u003c-- internal/protocol/protocol.go:184\n        ├── validateCacheMetadata() \u003c-- 2d\n        │   └── Check cache source has cache_key \u003c-- internal/protocol/protocol.go:195\n        ├── validateMemoryMetadata() \u003c-- 2e\n        │   └── Check memory source has name \u0026 type \u003c-- internal/protocol/protocol.go:211\n        └── validateErrorStatusCode() \u003c-- 2f\n            ├── extractStatusCode() \u003c-- internal/protocol/protocol.go:311\n            └── Verify 400-599 range for errors \u003c-- internal/protocol/protocol.go:317",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nEvery skill execution in foxctl produces a **JSON envelope** as output—a standardized wrapper containing the result, metadata, and error information. The problem: skills can produce malformed envelopes (missing timestamps, wrong status codes, inconsistent error fields), and downstream consumers need to trust this data for caching, persistence, and observability. **Protocol validation** [2a] ensures that every envelope conforms to the Core Profile v1 specification before it's written to output, stored in the job database, or returned to users.\n\nWithout validation, a skill could return `status: \"ok\"` with error fields populated, or set `meta.source = \"cache\"` without providing a cache key—breaking assumptions throughout the system and causing silent failures in caching, memory retrieval, and artifact management.\n\n## Details\n\n**Two-layer validation architecture**: The system has a base layer (`internal/domain/envelope`) that validates Core Profile v1 invariants [2b], and a protocol layer (`internal/protocol`) that adds domain-specific consistency checks [2a].\n\n**Base envelope validation** [2b] enforces the fundamental contract:\n- Version must be 1\n- Status must be \"ok\" or \"error\" \n- Command must be non-empty\n- Timestamp must be RFC3339 UTC format\n- **Status-specific rules**: OK envelopes cannot have error fields; error envelopes must have both error.code and error.message\n\n**Protocol-level extensions** add four consistency checks:\n\n1. **CAS digest matching** [2c]: If `data.artifact` exists (pointing to content-addressable storage), and `meta.cas_digest` is set, they must match exactly. The artifact field must use the `sha256:` prefix. This prevents cache corruption and ensures artifact references are valid.\n\n2. **Cache metadata consistency** [2d]: If `meta.source = \"cache\"`, then `meta.cache_key` must be present. This ensures cache hits are traceable and debuggable.\n\n3. **Memory metadata consistency** [2e]: If `meta.source = \"memory\"`, then `meta.memory` must contain both `name` and `type` fields. This ensures memory retrievals can be properly attributed.\n\n4. **Error status code validation** [2f]: Error envelopes with `data.summary.status_code` must have codes in the 400-599 range. This catches bugs where skills return HTTP 200 status codes in error envelopes.\n\n**Enforcement points**: Validation runs at every boundary—when writing envelopes to output [5a], when persisting job results [6c], and when receiving daemon responses [8b]. Invalid envelopes are rejected before they can corrupt the system state."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "title": "CAS Digest Validation with Reflection-Based Extraction",
      "description": "Protocol package - deep dive into artifact field extraction using reflection to handle various data types",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "3a",
          "path": "internal/protocol/protocol.go",
          "lineNumber": 163,
          "lineContent": "func validateCASDigest(env envelope.Envelope) error {",
          "title": "CAS Digest Validator",
          "description": "Validates consistency between meta.cas_digest and data.artifact fields"
        },
        {
          "id": "3b",
          "path": "internal/protocol/protocol.go",
          "lineNumber": 164,
          "lineContent": "artifactStr, ok, err := extractArtifactValue(env.Data)",
          "title": "Artifact Extraction Call",
          "description": "Extracts artifact field from polymorphic data payload"
        },
        {
          "id": "3c",
          "path": "internal/protocol/protocol.go",
          "lineNumber": 222,
          "lineContent": "func extractArtifactValue(data any) (string, bool, error) {",
          "title": "Artifact Extractor Entry",
          "description": "Handles multiple data types: maps, structs, json.RawMessage using reflection"
        },
        {
          "id": "3d",
          "path": "internal/protocol/protocol.go",
          "lineNumber": 266,
          "lineContent": "case reflect.Map:",
          "title": "Map-Based Extraction",
          "description": "Iterates map keys to find 'artifact' field using reflection"
        },
        {
          "id": "3e",
          "path": "internal/protocol/protocol.go",
          "lineNumber": 176,
          "lineContent": "if !strings.HasPrefix(artifactStr, \"sha256:\") {",
          "title": "Digest Prefix Validation",
          "description": "Enforces sha256: prefix requirement for artifact digests"
        },
        {
          "id": "3f",
          "path": "internal/protocol/protocol.go",
          "lineNumber": 184,
          "lineContent": "if env.Meta.CASDigest != artifactStr {",
          "title": "Digest Mismatch Check",
          "description": "Returns error if meta.cas_digest doesn't match data.artifact"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "CAS Digest Validation Flow\n├── validateCASDigest() entry \u003c-- 3a\n│   ├── extractArtifactValue(env.Data) \u003c-- 3b\n│   │   └── extractArtifactValue() dispatcher \u003c-- 3c\n│   │       ├── Handle json.RawMessage \u003c-- internal/protocol/protocol.go:227\n│   │       ├── Handle map[string]string \u003c-- internal/protocol/protocol.go:240\n│   │       ├── Handle map[string]any\n│   │       └── Reflection fallback \u003c-- internal/protocol/protocol.go:252\n│   │           ├── reflect.Map case \u003c-- 3d\n│   │           │   └── Iterate keys for \"artifact\" \u003c-- internal/protocol/protocol.go:267\n│   │           └── reflect.Struct case \u003c-- internal/protocol/protocol.go:291\n│   │               └── FieldByName(\"Artifact\") \u003c-- internal/protocol/protocol.go:292\n│   ├── Check sha256: prefix \u003c-- 3e\n│   └── Compare digests \u003c-- 3f\n│       └── Return error if mismatch \u003c-- internal/protocol/protocol.go:185\n└── Called from protocol.Validate() \u003c-- internal/protocol/protocol.go:142\n    └── Part of protocol validation chain",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nThe foxctl system uses a **canonical JSON envelope format** to wrap all I/O, ensuring structured and validated communication between skills, agents, and the CLI. A critical requirement is that when large outputs are stored in the **Content-Addressable Storage (CAS)**, the envelope must maintain consistency: if `data.artifact` contains a digest like `\"sha256:abc123\"`, and `meta.cas_digest` is also set, they **must match exactly** [3a]. This prevents silent data corruption where metadata points to the wrong artifact.\n\nThe challenge is that `data` can be **any JSON-compatible type**: a map, a struct, raw JSON bytes, or deeply nested objects. The validation system needs to **extract the artifact field** from this polymorphic payload and verify digest consistency, regardless of how the data is structured.\n\n## Details\n\n**Validation Entry Point** [3a]\n\nThe `validateCASDigest()` function is called as part of the protocol-level validation chain. It enforces the rule: if both `data.artifact` and `meta.cas_digest` exist, they must be identical. If only `data.artifact` exists, that's valid (digest is optional). If `meta.cas_digest` is set but no artifact exists, that's an error.\n\n**Polymorphic Extraction** [3b, 3c]\n\nThe `extractArtifactValue()` function handles the polymorphism problem. It accepts `data any` and returns `(string, bool, error)` where the bool indicates whether an artifact field was found. The function uses a **cascading type check strategy**:\n\n1. **Fast paths for common types**: Checks `json.RawMessage`, `map[string]string`, and `map[string]any` first using type assertions\n2. **Reflection fallback** [3d]: For unknown types, uses Go's `reflect` package to introspect the value at runtime\n\n**Reflection-Based Map Handling** [3d]\n\nWhen the data is a map type (detected via `reflect.Map`), the code iterates through all map keys looking for one named `\"artifact\"`. This handles cases where the map's concrete type isn't known at compile time. The iteration extracts the value and recursively calls `extractArtifactValue()` if needed (e.g., if the value is itself a `json.RawMessage`).\n\n**Digest Format Validation** [3e]\n\nOnce an artifact string is extracted, the code enforces that it **must start with `\"sha256:\"`**. This is a Core Profile v1 requirement—all CAS digests use SHA-256 with the explicit prefix.\n\n**Consistency Check** [3f]\n\nFinally, if `meta.cas_digest` is non-empty, it's compared against the extracted artifact value. A mismatch returns a descriptive error like `\"meta.cas_digest (sha256:abc) does not match data.artifact (sha256:xyz)\"`, preventing the envelope from being written or processed further.\n\nThis validation runs **before every envelope write** [5a, 5b] and at critical boundaries like skill execution results [6c] and daemon responses [8b], ensuring the system never emits or accepts inconsistent envelopes."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "title": "Error Envelope Creation with Typed Error Codes",
      "description": "Protocol package - shows how error envelopes are constructed with canonical error codes from Core Profile v1",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "4a",
          "path": "internal/protocol/protocol.go",
          "lineNumber": 125,
          "lineContent": "func Error(command string, code ErrorCode, message string, data any, opts ...Option) envelope.Envelope {",
          "title": "Protocol Error Constructor",
          "description": "Builds error envelope with typed error code and options"
        },
        {
          "id": "4b",
          "path": "internal/protocol/protocol.go",
          "lineNumber": 126,
          "lineContent": "env := envelope.Error(command, string(code), message, data)",
          "title": "Base Error Envelope Creation",
          "description": "Delegates to base envelope.Error with string code conversion"
        },
        {
          "id": "4c",
          "path": "internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go",
          "lineNumber": 162,
          "lineContent": "func Error(command, code, message string, data any, opts ...Option) Envelope {",
          "title": "Base Error Constructor",
          "description": "Core envelope error constructor with status, error fields, and timestamp"
        },
        {
          "id": "4d",
          "path": "internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go",
          "lineNumber": 171,
          "lineContent": "Error: ErrorFields{",
          "title": "Error Fields Assignment",
          "description": "Sets error.code and error.message in envelope structure"
        },
        {
          "id": "4e",
          "path": "internal/protocol/errors.go",
          "lineNumber": 7,
          "lineContent": "ErrorCodeEARG ErrorCode = \"EARG\"",
          "title": "Error Code Constants",
          "description": "Canonical error codes defined as typed constants (EARG, EAUTH, ERUNTIME, etc.)"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Error Envelope Creation Flow\n├── Protocol Layer Entry\n│   └── protocol.Error() \u003c-- 4a\n│       └── envelope.Error() call \u003c-- 4b\n│\n├── Base Envelope Layer\n│   └── envelope.Error() constructor \u003c-- 4c\n│       ├── Version = 1 \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:164\n│       ├── Status = \"error\" \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:165\n│       ├── Command = command \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:166\n│       ├── Data = data \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:167\n│       ├── Meta.TS = RFC3339 UTC \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:169\n│       └── ErrorFields assignment \u003c-- 4d\n│           ├── Code = code (string) \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:172\n│           └── Message = message \u003c-- internal/domain/envelope/envelope.go:173\n│\n└── Error Code Constants\n    └── Typed ErrorCode definitions \u003c-- 4e\n        ├── EARG = \"EARG\" \u003c-- internal/protocol/errors.go:8\n        ├── EAUTH = \"EAUTH\" \u003c-- internal/protocol/errors.go:12\n        ├── ERUNTIME = \"ERUNTIME\" \u003c-- internal/protocol/errors.go:18\n        └── [other canonical codes...]",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nagentctl uses a **canonical JSON envelope format** for all I/O to ensure structured, predictable communication between skills, agents, and the CLI. Every operation—whether successful or failed—must return a valid envelope with specific required fields. The problem: skills are written by different authors, run in different isolation modes (WASI, exec, OCI), and need to communicate errors in a **standardized way** that LLMs and automation can reliably parse. Without typed error codes and strict validation, the system would have no way to distinguish between \"invalid argument\" (retry won't help) and \"rate limit exceeded\" (retry after delay).\n\n## Details\n\n### Error Envelope Structure\n\nError envelopes follow the Core Profile v1 specification with three critical components:\n\n1. **Status field** must be `\"error\"` [4c]\n2. **Error fields** with `code` and `message` [4d]\n3. **Metadata** with RFC3339 UTC timestamp [4c]\n\n### Two-Layer Construction\n\nThe protocol package provides a **typed wrapper** around the base envelope package [4a]. When creating an error:\n\n- `protocol.Error()` accepts a typed `ErrorCode` constant [4a]\n- It converts the typed code to a string and delegates to `envelope.Error()` [4b]\n- The base constructor builds the envelope structure with all required fields [4c]\n\nThis two-layer design separates **domain types** (base envelope package) from **protocol-level helpers** (typed error codes, validation extensions).\n\n### Canonical Error Codes\n\nThe system defines **15+ canonical error codes** as typed constants [4e], including:\n\n- **EARG** - Invalid arguments or validation errors [4e]\n- **EAUTH** - Authentication failures\n- **ERUNTIME** - Execution failures, crashes, 5xx errors\n- **ETIMEOUT** - Operation timeouts\n- **ENOTFOUND** - Resource not found\n\nThese codes map to HTTP status ranges and enable **automated retry logic**—the `IsRetryable()` function knows that ERUNTIME and ETIMEOUT warrant retries, while EARG does not.\n\n### Why Typed Constants Matter\n\nUsing `ErrorCode` types instead of raw strings provides:\n\n1. **Compile-time safety** - typos caught at build time\n2. **IDE autocomplete** - developers discover valid codes\n3. **Centralized definitions** - single source of truth in `errors.go` [4e]\n\nThe envelope itself stores codes as strings for JSON compatibility, but the protocol layer enforces type safety at construction boundaries."
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "title": "Envelope Writing with Validation Enforcement",
